  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a constructive approach based on pattern mixing and phaselock optimization to improve over the existing solution. The current method uses sequential refinement starting from an initial candidate, which may not be optimal. Our idea is to:\n- Generate several candidate functions by combining different base patterns (impulse-based, sinusoidal, block) under a periodic constraint\n- Perform optimization on mixed-pattern constructions to preserve diversity while maintaining sum constraint\n- Develop synthetic heuristics that ensure better distribution of peak overlaps\n\nThe key bottleneck addressed is the limited search space of the original method without changing periodic structure. We focus on relative structures that prevent correlation artifacts from propagating through the domain.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization domain: Step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with \u222bh(x)dx=1.\nDiscretization: n_point samples with dx=2/n_points, so \u2211h_i = n_points/2.\n\nLet F(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx (approximately represented by discrete values)\n\nConstraints:\n- For each i, 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n\nRepresentation:\n- Use periodic boundary matching for correlation to ensure domain consistency\n- Employ relative coordinate shifting for optimization\n\nOptimization:\n- Maximize min-correlation over periodic matches\n- Use projected ADMM for distributed updates in the mixer phase\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInitialize parent/child structures:\n- Start with initial_h_values as fallback parent\n- Define mixers: \n    - mixer_1: impulse mixing with Gaussian blurring\n    - mixer_2: sinusoidal mixing with beat tuning\n    - mixer_3: block mixing with phase offsets\n- Create mixed_candidate(layer, mixing_depth)\n\nCore phases:\nPhase A: Low-dimensional seeding\n- Generate candidates by low-frequency convolution of base patterns\n- Match sum constraint with projected descent\n- Keep best candidate modulo l1-regularization\n\nPhase B: Progressive optimization\n- Single-particle crossover: blend two solutions with differential weighting\n- Use gradient-based local search with periodicity preservation\n- Maintain diversity trough annealed reversible exchanges\n\nPhase C: Ultra-fine polishing\n- Directed mutation by lag-space coherence checking\n- Use lookahead voting on k-space correlation reductions\n- Dynamic repair ensuring h[i] continuity\n\nDetailed steps (n_points=560 as optimal resolution):\n\n1. Parent validation:\n   h0 = initial_h_values\n   if sum(h0)*dx != 1: repair_parent(h0)\n\n2. Initialization:\n   n = 560 if parent resolution allows else 280\n   dx = 2/n\n   candidate = {}\n   for mixer in [mixer_1, mixer_2, mixer_3]:\n        cand = construct_candidate(mixer)\n        cand = projected_admm(cand, target_sum=0.5*n*dx)\n        store improvement\n\n3. Progressive mixing loop:\n   While time < MAX_TIME - 100s:\n        new_cand = admm_mix(best[0], best[1], alpha=0.95)\n        new_cand = directed_mutation(new_cand)\n        if evaluate_overlap(new_cand) < best_overlap:\n            best = (new_cand, evaluate_overlap(new_cand))\n\n4. Diversity maintenance:\n   Maintain two parallel solutions:\n        primary = deterministic improvement path\n        secondary = diversity random set\n\n5. Final phase:\n   For lag in range(0, n-1):\n        if crossover_point(lag) > threshold:\n            target_shape_dir(lag)\n   Final repair and consistency check\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Basic variation: Use 560-point resolution initially\n- Exploration variation: Random restart every 100-sectored diversity grid\n- Exploitation variation: Progressive ADMM relaxation with momentum\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Progressive optimization estimated: \n    - Mixed candidate generation: ~30s\n    - Local search per candidate: ~50s * number of candidates\n    - Final polishing: ~200s\n- Use early termination if candidate gradient shows no improvement\n- Repair minimums: ensure 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 via blending\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Always repair candidate to ensure feasibility\n- Evaluate overlaping integral using:\n    correlation = np.correlate(h, 1-h, 'full')\n    C5 = np.max(correlation) * dx\n- Return modified time-critical multi-stage optimization with:\n    \u2022 Progressive mixing to maintain diversity\n    \u2022 Dynamic resolution switching between 280, 560, and 1120\n    \u2022 Violation-based pruning under resource constraints\n    \u2022 Heredity checks ensuring genetic continuity",
